{
  "requirement": "`fingerprint` identifies the version of the contents. `format` renders that same value for the agent.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait NodeContents: Send + Sync {\n    fn fingerprint(&self) -> Fingerprint;\n    fn format(&self) -> String;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only structural obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.NodeContents` is the prescribed trait declaration: `pub trait NodeContents: Send + Sync` with `fn fingerprint(&self) -> Fingerprint` and `fn format(&self) -> String`. The sentence \"`fingerprint` identifies the version of the contents. `format` renders that same value\" is behavioral. It depends on each implementor, and the trait itself can't enforce it.\n\nI checked the root `crates/trust-but-verify/src/domain.rs#trait[NodeContents]`. It declares exactly this trait: the `Send + Sync` supertraits, `fn fingerprint(&self) -> Fingerprint` and `fn format(&self) -> String`. The root carries the `{impl}` marker for this requirement. Its two methods carry markers for the child requirements `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint` and `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`. Those child requirements are assessed on their own.\n\nA behavior-preserving alternative that breaks the clause would be a trait without `Send + Sync`, a `format` that returns `&str`, or a `fingerprint` that returns a different type. The root has none of these, so it covers the structural obligation and nothing else needs a marker.\n\nbehavioral: The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.NodeContents` has two behavioral obligations. Both are covered by the marked tests, and no gap remains.\n\n1. `fingerprint` identifies the version of the contents.\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[structural_fingerprint_example_tracks_the_selected_function]` shows that a reformatted function has an equal fingerprint. It also shows that a changed body (`deny()` in place of `allow()`) has an unequal fingerprint.\n   - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[selected_contents_outlive_handles_and_later_file_edits]` shows that the fingerprint stays equal to the original after the file is rewritten. It also checks the fingerprint's value and scheme against the structural hash and the fingerprint scheme.\n\n2. `format` renders that same value for the agent.\n   - Both tests assert `format()` output exactly. It is `\"second\"` before and after the file edit. For the three function variants it is the original source text of each one, including the reformatted whitespace.\n   - The rendering follows the same retained value that is fingerprinted. It stays stable when the file changes, and it changes when the selected contents change.\n\nThe trait's `Send + Sync` bound is checked in two places. The structural test coerces `&dyn NodeContents` to a `Send + Sync` reference. `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs` also has a bounds check. The `public_exports_match_the_appendix` test is marked, and it covers the exported trait inventory.\n\nI did not read implementation source.",
